WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.218
JavaScript applications process untrusted data constantly.

00:00:03.218 --> 00:00:11.156
A browser application receives form fields, query strings, storage values, messages, files, and data from third-party services.

00:00:11.156 --> 00:00:12.658
A Node.

00:00:12.658 --> 00:00:21.025
js service receives request bodies, route parameters, headers, cookies, WebSocket messages, environment variables, queue events, and API responses.

00:00:21.025 --> 00:00:31.967
Input validation is the defensive boundary that asks whether a value is acceptable before the application trusts it or uses it to make decisions.

00:00:31.967 --> 00:00:36.902
JavaScript's flexibility is productive, but it also creates validation risk.

00:00:36.902 --> 00:00:45.269
Dynamic typing and type coercion can make values behave in unexpected ways if checks are loose or ambiguous.

00:00:45.269 --> 00:01:01.789
A string may be compared like a number, an empty value may become false, an array may be accepted where an object was expected, and a missing field may move through code until it causes unsafe behavior.

00:01:01.789 --> 00:01:05.436
Clear runtime validation makes those assumptions explicit.

00:01:05.436 --> 00:01:11.658
Validation is related to other controls, but it is not the same thing.

00:01:11.658 --> 00:01:15.305
Parsing turns raw input into structured data.

00:01:15.305 --> 00:01:18.952
Normalization converts data into a consistent representation.

00:01:18.952 --> 00:01:23.887
Sanitization changes data, often to remove or transform unwanted parts.

00:01:23.887 --> 00:01:32.254
Output encoding prepares data for a specific output context such as HTML, JavaScript, URLs, logs, or SQL parameters.

00:01:32.254 --> 00:01:37.618
Validation decides whether the application should accept the value at all.

00:01:37.618 --> 00:01:42.123
Input validation is one layer of a secure design.

00:01:42.123 --> 00:01:50.919
It does not replace authorization, output encoding, parameterized queries, safe file handling, safe command execution, or business logic checks.

00:01:50.919 --> 00:01:53.279
Its value is predictability.

00:01:53.279 --> 00:02:01.647
When JavaScript applications validate inputs at the right boundaries, they reduce surprises before untrusted data reaches sensitive code.
